---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "How Much Electricity Does a Treadmill Use? (2026) — Real Numbers";
const description =
  "A home treadmill draws 300 to 900 watts in use and costs most households under 4 dollars a month. Here is the arithmetic, what actually moves the number, and the standby draw nobody mentions.";

const COSTS = [
  {
    profile: "Walking pad, 60 min/day at 3 mph",
    draw: "~180 W",
    cost: "About $0.90 per month",
  },
  {
    profile: "Home treadmill, 30 min/day walking",
    draw: "~450 W",
    cost: "About $1.15 per month",
  },
  {
    profile: "Home treadmill, 45 min/day jogging",
    draw: "~750 W",
    cost: "About $2.85 per month",
  },
  {
    profile: "Home treadmill, 60 min/day running at incline",
    draw: "~1,100 W",
    cost: "About $5.60 per month",
  },
  {
    profile: "Commercial AC machine, 60 min/day",
    draw: "~1,500 W",
    cost: "About $7.60 per month",
  },
  {
    profile: "Standby only, plugged in and idle",
    draw: "2 – 8 W",
    cost: "About $0.10 – $0.40 per month",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Electricity Usage", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-electricity-usage/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much electricity does a treadmill use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A home treadmill draws roughly 300 to 900 watts while running, depending on the motor, your weight and your pace. At the US average of about 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, 45 minutes a day of jogging costs around 3 dollars a month. Walking costs about a third of that. It is a small line on a household bill by any measure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I calculate my treadmill's running cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Multiply the watts drawn by the hours used to get watt-hours, divide by 1,000 for kilowatt-hours, then multiply by your electricity rate. A 750-watt machine used 45 minutes a day is 0.56 kWh per day, about 17 kWh a month, or roughly 2.85 dollars at 17 cents per kWh. Use the label figure only as a ceiling, since it states maximum draw rather than typical.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a treadmill use a lot of electricity compared to other appliances?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A treadmill in use draws about the same as a microwave or a hairdryer, but it runs for well under an hour a day rather than continuously. A refrigerator, a tumble dryer, an electric water heater and air conditioning all cost a household far more per year than a home treadmill does.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does using incline increase treadmill electricity use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, in two ways. The incline motor itself draws power while it is changing grade, though only briefly. More significantly, running at incline means the drive motor is lifting your body weight as well as moving the belt, which raises the sustained draw. A steep incline walk can use more power than a flat jog for the same person.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I unplug my treadmill when not in use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For the electricity saving alone, it is not worth it — standby is typically 2 to 8 watts, which is a few cents a month. There is a better reason to unplug: it protects the controller board from power surges, which is a common cause of a treadmill that will not turn on. Use a surge protector rated for the machine's draw if you would rather leave it connected.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a treadmill need its own circuit?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most home treadmills are fine on a standard grounded 15-amp household circuit provided nothing else heavy is sharing it. Manufacturers commonly recommend a dedicated 20-amp circuit for larger machines, and most explicitly advise against extension leads, because start-up current draw is much higher than running draw and a marginal connection heats up.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-09-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-06",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill electricity",
      "treadmill running cost",
      "treadmill watts",
      "home gym running costs",
      "treadmill power",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-elec" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-elec)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Owner's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How Much <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Electricity</span> Does a Treadmill Use?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Less than almost everyone assumes. A home treadmill in daily use adds
          somewhere between one and six dollars a month to a typical bill — but
          the wattage on the label is not the number to calculate with, and the
          circuit it is plugged into matters more than the cost.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#costs" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Costs
          </a>
          <a href="#circuits" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Circuits and Safety
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 6, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">The short answer.</strong> A home treadmill
          draws 300 to 900 watts in use. Forty-five minutes a day of jogging works
          out to roughly 3 dollars a month at the US average rate of about 17 cents
          per kilowatt-hour. Running costs are not a reason to choose one treadmill
          over another, and they are not a reason to avoid buying one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-maths" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Calculation in One Line</a>
          <a href="#costs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Monthly Cost by Use Pattern</a>
          <a href="#label" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Why the Label Wattage Misleads</a>
          <a href="#variables" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What Actually Moves the Number</a>
          <a href="#standby" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Standby Draw and Unplugging</a>
          <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Compared With Other Appliances</a>
          <a href="#circuits" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Circuits, Extension Leads and Surges</a>
          <a href="#reducing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Genuinely Reducing Consumption</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This question is usually asked in the wrong spirit. People want to know
          whether a treadmill will noticeably raise the bill, and the answer is no
          — a few dollars a month for most households. The question worth asking
          instead is what the machine is plugged into, because the electrical
          detail that matters is not cost, it is start-up current and surge
          protection.
        </p>

        <div id="the-maths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Calculation in One Line</h2>
          <p>
            Watts times hours, divided by a thousand, times your rate. That is the
            whole thing, and doing it once for your own machine is more useful than
            any average.
          </p>
          <p>
            Take a machine drawing 750 watts, used 45 minutes a day. That is 750
            multiplied by 0.75 hours, which is 563 watt-hours, or 0.56 kilowatt-
            hours per day. Over a 30-day month that is about 17 kWh. At the US
            residential average of roughly 17 cents per kWh in 2026, the monthly
            cost is around 2.85 dollars, and the annual cost around 34 dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your own rate is the variable that moves this most, and it varies
            enormously — from around 11 cents per kWh in parts of the South to over
            30 cents in Hawaii, California and much of New England. Check the rate
            on your own bill rather than using a national figure, because the
            difference between 11 and 30 cents nearly triples every number on this
            page.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other variable is honesty about usage. Most people calculate for
            the training they intend to do rather than the training they do. If the
            machine gets used three times a week rather than daily, halve
            everything here. That is not a criticism — it is the single largest
            error in every home-gym running-cost estimate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Monthly Cost by Use Pattern</h2>
          <p>
            Figures below assume 17 cents per kilowatt-hour and thirty days of use
            per month. Draw figures are typical sustained draw for a user around
            180 lb, not the peak on the machine's rating plate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Machine and Use</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Typical Draw</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Cost at 17c/kWh</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              COSTS.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.profile}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.draw}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.cost}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The spread between the cheapest and the most expensive row is about seven
          dollars a month. That is the entire range of the question, from a walking
          pad to a commercial AC machine run for an hour daily. For context, it is
          roughly what a single household tumble-dryer cycle costs per week.
        </p>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why the Label Wattage Misleads</h2>
          <p>
            The rating plate on the frame or under the motor hood states the
            machine's maximum draw, and it is typically 1,500 to 2,000 watts on a
            home treadmill. Calculating from that number produces a cost estimate
            two to four times too high, which is why so many published figures for
            this question are wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason for the gap is that a motor draws power in proportion to the
            work it is doing, not in proportion to what it could do. A 3.0 CHP
            motor moving a 160 lb walker at 3 mph on a well-lubricated deck is
            producing a small fraction of its rated output and drawing accordingly.
            The rating plate is an electrical safety figure — it tells an
            electrician what the circuit must tolerate — rather than a consumption
            figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            The distinction is the same one that runs through our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>:
            peak capacity and sustained output are different quantities, and the
            marketing consistently quotes the one that flatters.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a real number for your own machine, a plug-in energy
            monitor costs very little and settles the question in one session. It
            will also show you something more interesting than the total, which is
            how sharply the draw changes with incline and pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="variables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Actually Moves the Number</h2>
          <p>
            Four things change consumption meaningfully, and two of them are within
            your control.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Your weight.</strong> The motor is working against the friction
            between belt and deck, and that friction scales with the load pressing
            them together. A 250 lb user draws appreciably more than a 150 lb user
            at the same speed on the same machine. This is the largest single
            variable and it is not adjustable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> Larger than most people expect. At grade, the
            motor is not only moving the belt but contributing to lifting your mass
            against gravity, and the draw rises steeply. A 12 per cent walk can
            consume more than a flat jog for the same person, which is worth
            knowing if you follow the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 protocol</a>
            daily.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck condition.</strong> This one is adjustable and it is the
            reason maintenance appears in an article about electricity. A dry deck
            raises friction substantially, and the motor compensates by drawing
            more current to hold the set speed. A neglected machine can use
            noticeably more power than a maintained one doing identical work — and
            the extra energy is going into heat, which is what kills controllers.
            Ten minutes with the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            is the only energy-saving measure here that is genuinely worth doing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt tension.</strong> The same logic. An over-tightened belt
            adds continuous drag the motor must overcome, raising both draw and
            heat. It is one of several reasons over-tensioning is a costly habit,
            covered in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Standby Draw and Unplugging</h2>
          <p>
            A treadmill plugged in but switched off still draws a small amount to
            keep the console alive and, on connected machines, the wireless radio
            listening. Typical figures are 2 to 8 watts, which over a month is
            roughly 1.5 to 6 kWh — call it 25 cents to a dollar.
          </p>
          <p>
            Purely as an economy measure, unplugging is not worth the walk across
            the room. Smart-console machines sit at the higher end of that range,
            and if a machine is unused for months the standby total becomes
            slightly more noticeable, but it is still small.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a much better reason to unplug, and it has nothing to do with
            the bill. The controller board is the component most likely to fail on
            a home treadmill, and mains surges are one of the ways it fails. A
            machine that sits unused for weeks is exposed to every storm and grid
            event in that period for no benefit at all. Unplugging is cheap
            insurance on the most expensive part.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you would rather leave it connected, use a surge protector rated for
            the machine's current draw rather than a basic power strip. And note
            that a surge protector is not the same thing as an extension lead used
            to reach a distant outlet, which is a different and worse idea — see
            the next section. A treadmill that has stopped responding entirely is
            often a board that took a hit, which we work through in
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill will not turn on</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Compared With Other Appliances</h2>
          <p>
            The useful framing is not watts but watt-hours, because duration
            dominates. A treadmill has a high draw and a very short duty cycle,
            which is the opposite profile to most of what raises a household bill.
          </p>
          <p>
            In use, a treadmill draws about what a microwave or a hairdryer draws.
            Nobody worries about the running cost of a hairdryer, and for the same
            reason — it is on for minutes. A treadmill used 45 minutes a day is a
            hairdryer used 45 minutes a day, and the annual figure lands around 30
            to 40 dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            The genuinely expensive things in a house all share the property of
            running for a long time or working against a large temperature
            difference: air conditioning, electric water heating, a tumble dryer, a
            refrigerator that runs 24 hours a day at low draw. Any one of those
            costs a multiple of the treadmill's annual consumption.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one genuinely fair comparison is against a gym membership, which is
            what most people are really weighing. A home treadmill's electricity is
            roughly a tenth of one month of typical gym fees per year. Whatever the
            case for or against home equipment, the power bill is not part of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="circuits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Circuits, Extension Leads and Surges</h2>
          <p>
            This is the part of the electrical question that actually matters, and
            it is not about money.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start-up current is much higher than running current. When a stationary
            belt with a person on it begins to move, the motor briefly demands
            several times its steady draw. That momentary demand is what
            manufacturers size their circuit recommendations around, and it is why
            a machine can run happily for months and then trip a breaker the first
            time someone starts it while standing on the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most home treadmills are fine on a standard grounded 15-amp circuit
            provided nothing else substantial shares it. Larger machines,
            particularly AC-motored
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial-grade treadmills</a>,
            commonly specify a dedicated 20-amp circuit. Check the manual, because
            this is one of the few manufacturer recommendations with a real
            engineering basis behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nearly every manufacturer advises against extension leads, and the
            reason is worth understanding rather than dismissing. A thin or long
            lead has enough resistance to drop voltage under a heavy start-up
            draw, which makes the motor pull more current to compensate, which
            heats the lead. It is a slow, self-reinforcing problem, and it is a
            genuine fire risk rather than a liability disclaimer. If the machine
            cannot reach an outlet, the correct fix is to move the machine or add
            an outlet.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more habit worth adopting: start the belt before you step on it,
            not while standing on it. It reduces the start-up load considerably,
            and on a marginal circuit it is the difference between running and
            tripping the breaker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reducing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Genuinely Reducing Consumption</h2>
          <p>
            Given the totals involved, most energy-saving advice for treadmills is
            not worth the effort. Three things are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the deck lubricated.</strong> This is the only measure
            with a meaningful effect, and its real benefit is not the electricity —
            it is that the extra current a dry deck draws becomes heat inside the
            motor and controller, which is how machines die. The energy saving is a
            side effect of the maintenance being worth doing anyway.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not over-tension the belt.</strong> Same mechanism, same
            small saving, same much larger benefit in component life. A belt you can
            lift two to three inches at the centre of the deck is correctly
            tensioned.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use incline deliberately rather than by default.</strong>
            Incline is the largest controllable variable in the consumption
            calculation. This is not a reason to avoid incline training, which is
            genuinely effective — it is simply worth knowing that a permanent
            2 per cent default costs power for a benefit you may not be seeking.
          </p>
          <p>
            What is not worth doing: buying a smaller motor to save power. A larger
            motor doing the same work draws similar current and runs cooler doing
            it, so the underpowered machine is not the economical choice on any
            timescale that matters. And manual treadmills, which use no electricity
            at all, are a genuinely different training experience rather than an
            efficiency upgrade — the full ownership picture is in
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does a treadmill use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A home treadmill draws roughly 300 to 900 watts while running,
                depending on the motor, your weight and your pace. At the US
                average of about 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, 45 minutes a day of
                jogging costs around 3 dollars a month. Walking costs about a third
                of that. It is a small line on a household bill by any measure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate my treadmill's running cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply the watts drawn by the hours used to get watt-hours,
                divide by 1,000 for kilowatt-hours, then multiply by your
                electricity rate. A 750-watt machine used 45 minutes a day is 0.56
                kWh per day, about 17 kWh a month, or roughly 2.85 dollars at 17
                cents per kWh. Use the label figure only as a ceiling, since it
                states maximum draw rather than typical.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill use a lot of electricity compared to other appliances?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A treadmill in use draws about the same as a microwave or a
                hairdryer, but it runs for well under an hour a day rather than
                continuously. A refrigerator, a tumble dryer, an electric water
                heater and air conditioning all cost a household far more per year
                than a home treadmill does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does using incline increase treadmill electricity use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in two ways. The incline motor itself draws power while it is
                changing grade, though only briefly. More significantly, running at
                incline means the drive motor is lifting your body weight as well
                as moving the belt, which raises the sustained draw. A steep
                incline walk can use more power than a flat jog for the same
                person.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I unplug my treadmill when not in use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the electricity saving alone, it is not worth it — standby is
                typically 2 to 8 watts, which is a few cents a month. There is a
                better reason to unplug: it protects the controller board from
                power surges, which is a common cause of a treadmill that will not
                turn on. Use a surge protector rated for the machine's draw if you
                would rather leave it connected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill need its own circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most home treadmills are fine on a standard grounded 15-amp
                household circuit provided nothing else heavy is sharing it.
                Manufacturers commonly recommend a dedicated 20-amp circuit for
                larger machines, and most explicitly advise against extension
                leads, because start-up current draw is much higher than running
                draw and a marginal connection heats up.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes About Treadmill Power</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two produce wrong numbers. The last two produce dead controller boards.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calculating from the rating plate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 1,500 to 2,000 watts on the label is maximum draw for circuit-sizing purposes, not what the machine consumes in use. Estimates built on it come out two to four times too high, which is why so many published figures for this question are wrong. A plug-in energy monitor settles it for a few dollars.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring what a dry deck costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Friction is what the motor is fighting, so an unlubricated deck raises current draw at every speed. The wasted electricity is trivial; the heat it puts into the motor and the controller board sitting beside it is not. This is the one energy-saving measure worth doing, and it is worth doing for the other reason.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the machine on an extension lead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Start-up current is several times running current. A long or thin lead drops voltage under that load, the motor draws harder to compensate, and the lead heats. Manufacturers warn against this for engineering reasons rather than legal ones. If the outlet is out of reach, move the machine or add an outlet.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting the belt while standing on it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Accelerating a stationary belt with a person on it is the single largest current demand the machine ever makes, and on a marginal circuit it is what trips the breaker. Straddle the rails, start the belt, then step on. It is also easier on the drive belt and the controller.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

